msgid "10 minutes"
msgstr "10 minutes"

msgid "2 quizzes"
msgstr "2 quizzes"

msgid "A dictionary lists a verb in its -ma infinitive: <0/>, to live. Take off -ma and you have the stem, <1>ela-</1>. The ending goes on the stem: <2/>, I live."
msgstr "A dictionary lists a verb in its -ma infinitive: <0/>, to live. Take off -ma and you have the stem, <1>ela-</1>. The ending goes on the stem: <2/>, I live."

msgid "A dictionary lists a verb in its -ma infinitive: <0>elama</0>, to live. The endings go on its stem, <1>ela-</1>."
msgstr "A dictionary lists a verb in its -ma infinitive: <0>elama</0>, to live. The endings go on its stem, <1>ela-</1>."

msgid "All of them. You hear who does it."
msgstr "All of them. You hear who does it."

msgid "All six. You matched every pronoun with its ending."
msgstr "All six. You matched every pronoun with its ending."

msgid "As with olema, the ending already says who, so ma, sa, me and te often go: <0/>. To set two people side by side, use the long pronouns from lesson {pronouns}: <1/>."
msgstr "As with olema, the ending already says who, so ma, sa, me and te often go: <0/>. To set two people side by side, use the long pronouns from lesson {pronouns}: <1/>."

msgid "Each pronoun has its ending: <0>ma -n, sa -d, ta -b, me -me, te -te, nad -vad</0>, so <1>elan, elad, elab, elame, elate, elavad</1>."
msgstr "Each pronoun has its ending: <0>ma -n, sa -d, ta -b, me -me, te -te, nad -vad</0>, so <1>elan, elad, elab, elame, elate, elavad</1>."

msgid "Endings of elama"
msgstr "Endings of elama"

msgid "Estonian has one present tense. <0/> means both I read and I’m reading, and the rest of the sentence tells you which. Tap a sentence to hear it."
msgstr "Estonian has one present tense. <0/> means both I read and I’m reading, and the rest of the sentence tells you which. Tap a sentence to hear it."

msgid "Fill the ending"
msgstr "Fill the ending"

msgid "Go back to the endings above, then try again."
msgstr "Go back to the endings above, then try again."

msgid "he or she is; they are"
msgstr "he or she is; they are"

msgid "he or she lives"
msgstr "he or she lives"

msgid "He or she speaks Estonian."
msgstr "He or she speaks Estonian."

msgid "He or she works from home."
msgstr "He or she works from home."

msgid "Hear a sentence and pick the verb in it. The answers differ only in the ending, so listen to the end of the verb."
msgstr "Hear a sentence and pick the verb in it. The answers differ only in the ending, so listen to the end of the verb."

msgid "Here they are on <0/>, to live. The two new ones are in colour. Tap an ending."
msgstr "Here they are on <0/>, to live. The two new ones are in colour. Tap an ending."

msgid "I am"
msgstr "I am"

msgid "I drink coffee, he or she drinks water."
msgstr "I drink coffee, he or she drinks water."

msgid "I live"
msgstr "I live"

msgid "I live in Tallinn."
msgstr "I live in Tallinn."

msgid "I love you."
msgstr "I love you."

msgid "I read, I’m reading"
msgstr "I read, I’m reading"

msgid "I read; I’m reading"
msgstr "I read; I’m reading"

msgid "I’m learning Estonian."
msgstr "I’m learning Estonian."

msgid "I’m reading a book."
msgstr "I’m reading a book."

msgid "In lesson {pronouns} you met <0/>, to be: <1/>, <2/>, <3/>, <4/>. Most verbs take the same endings. Where olema says <5/>, they have two of their own: <6>-b</6> for he or she and <7>-vad</7> for they."
msgstr "In lesson {pronouns} you met <0/>, to be: <1/>, <2/>, <3/>, <4/>. Most verbs take the same endings. Where olema says <5/>, they have two of their own: <6>-b</6> for he or she and <7>-vad</7> for they."

msgid "In some verbs the stem changes: <0/>, to read, has the stem <1>loe-</1>, so I read is <2/>. You don’t need the rule for that yet. Learn each verb with its I form: <3>lugema, loen</3>. Take off -n and the rest is the stem. In lesson {verbForms} you’ll add a third form, <4>lugeda</4>."
msgstr "In some verbs the stem changes: <0/>, to read, has the stem <1>loe-</1>, so I read is <2/>. You don’t need the rule for that yet. Learn each verb with its I form: <3>lugema, loen</3>. Take off -n and the rest is the stem. In lesson {verbForms} you’ll add a third form, <4>lugeda</4>."

msgid "Listen to the very end of the verb, then try again."
msgstr "Listen to the very end of the verb, then try again."

msgid "Listening only, no microphone"
msgstr "Listening only, no microphone"

msgid "Long forms, because the two people are set side by side."
msgstr "Long forms, because the two people are set side by side."

msgid "More than one"
msgstr "More than one"

msgid "Most of them. Play the ones you missed once more."
msgstr "Most of them. Play the ones you missed once more."

msgid "Nearly. Tap the endings above once more."
msgstr "Nearly. Tap the endings above once more."

msgid "No ma: the ending -n already says I."
msgstr "No ma: the ending -n already says I."

msgid "One person"
msgstr "One person"

msgid "One present tense for both."
msgstr "One present tense for both."

msgid "One present tense: <0>ma loen</0> is both I read and I’m reading."
msgstr "One present tense: <0>ma loen</0> is both I read and I’m reading."

msgid "Pick the ending that goes with the pronoun, then hear the sentence."
msgstr "Pick the ending that goes with the pronoun, then hear the sentence."

msgid "Play {text}"
msgstr "Play {text}"

msgid "Six endings"
msgstr "Six endings"

msgid "Some stems change: <0>lugema, loen</0>. Learn each verb with its I form."
msgstr "Some stems change: <0>lugema, loen</0>. Learn each verb with its I form."

msgid "Stem {stem}-: ma {form}."
msgstr "Stem {stem}-: ma {form}."

msgid "Stem and ending"
msgstr "Stem and ending"

msgid "Stems such as <0>loe-</0> and <1>räägi-</1> lose or change a consonant inside the word. Lesson {gradation} explains the rule, called consonant gradation."
msgstr "Stems such as <0>loe-</0> and <1>räägi-</1> lose or change a consonant inside the word. Lesson {gradation} explains the rule, called consonant gradation."

msgid "Tallinnas ends in -s, which means in; lesson {where} explains that ending. Keelt and raamatut are in the partitive, a form an object often takes, which lesson {partitive} explains. For now, learn them as phrases."
msgstr "Tallinnas ends in -s, which means in; lesson {where} explains that ending. Keelt and raamatut are in the partitive, a form an object often takes, which lesson {partitive} explains. For now, learn them as phrases."

msgid "Tap an ending"
msgstr "Tap an ending"

msgid "Ten everyday verbs, the stem each one builds on, and six endings that say who does it."
msgstr "Ten everyday verbs, the stem each one builds on, and six endings that say who does it."

msgid "The ending for ma, I."
msgstr "The ending for ma, I."

msgid "The ending for me, we."
msgstr "The ending for me, we."

msgid "The ending for nad, they. Olema has on here too."
msgstr "The ending for nad, they. Olema has on here too."

msgid "The ending for sa, you."
msgstr "The ending for sa, you."

msgid "The ending for ta, he or she. Olema has on here."
msgstr "The ending for ta, he or she. Olema has on here."

msgid "The ending for te: you, to more than one; polite you."
msgstr "The ending for te: you, to more than one; polite you."

msgid "The ending says who, so ma, sa, me and te can go: <0>Elan Tallinnas.</0>"
msgstr "The ending says who, so ma, sa, me and te can go: <0>Elan Tallinnas.</0>"

msgid "They eat at home."
msgstr "They eat at home."

